<section xml:id="section-dihedral-groups">
  <title>Dihedral Groups, Symmetry Groups, Matrix Groups</title>
  <introduction>
    <p>
      Our first examples all started with numbers, and this makes
      sense: there are many groups made out of sets of numbers.
      However, many natural examples of groups come
      from geometric symmetries. If I think about symmetries as
      transformations and use composition of functions as the binary
      operation, I can make symmetry groups. I'll start with dihedral
      groups, which will be familiar to students who completed my
      linear algebra course. 
    </p>
  </introduction>
  <subsection xml:id="subsection-dihedral-groups">
    <title>Dihedral Groups</title>
    <definition>
      <statement>
        <p>
          A <term>Dihedral Group</term> is the group of linear
          symmetries of a regular polygon. If the polygon has <m>n</m>
          edges, then I will write <m>D_n</m> for the associated
          dihedral group. Thus, <m>D_3</m> is the group of symmetries
          of the triangle, <m>D_4</m> is the group of symmetries of
          the square, <m>D_5</m> is the group of symmetries of the
          pentagon, and so on. (Some texts use <m>D_{2n}</m> instead
          of <m>D_n</m>.)
        </p>
      </statement>
    </definition>
    <example xml:id="example-d4">
      <title>The Group <m>D_4</m></title>
      <figure xml:id="figure-d4">>
        <caption>Symmetries of the Square</caption>
        <image xml:id="image-d4" width="80%">
          <asymptote>
            size(10cm);
  
            dot((0,0));
            dot((0,2));
            dot((2,0));
            dot((2,2));
            draw((0,0)--(0,2)--(2,2)--(2,0)--(0,0));
            
            label("$1$",(2,2),NW);
            label("$2$",(0,2),NE);
            label("$3$",(0,0),SE);
            label("$4$",(2,0),SW);
  
            draw((-1,1)--(3,1));
            draw((1,-1)--(1,3));
            draw((-1,-1)--(3,3));
            draw((3,-1)--(-1,3));
            
            label("$F_1$",(3,1),N);
            label("$F_2$",(3,3),SE);
            label("$F_3$",(1,3),E);
            label("$F_4$",(-1,3),SW);
          </asymptote>
        </image>
      </figure>
      <p>
        I'll start with the symmetries of the square. I'll label the
        vertices of the square with the numbers <m>1</m> to <m>4</m>.
        I can describe all the symmetries of the square by simply
        indicating where the vertices go; the line between any two
        vertices is sent to the matching line between the image of the
        two vertices. 
      </p>
      <p>
        There are two relatively obvious types of symmetry that might
        occur to you.  First, there is rotation about the centre of
        the square. If I rotate by <m>\pi/2</m> radians (a quarter
        turn), the shape is preserved: the new square completely
        overlaps the old. Likewise for rotations by <m>\pi</m> and
        <m>3\pi/2</m> radians. If I am really paying attention, I
        might also think of rotation by <m>2\pi</m> radians, a full
        turn. This might seem silly, but this just describes the
        identity transformation (the transformation that leaves
        everything fixed), which is a part of any group and should be
        included in the list.  So, for now, the symmetries of the
        square include the identity and three rotations. I'll call
        these three rotations <m>R_1</m>, <m>R_2</m> and <m>R_3</m>,
        in the order I defined them.
      </p>
      <p>
        I might also think about reflections. Looking at the square, I
        can find four lines of reflection: the horizontal line through
        the middle, the vertical line through the middle, the line
        through vertices 1 and 3, and the line through vertices 2 and
        4.  I'd like to label the reflection by taking the lines of
        reflection in counter-clockwise order starting from the
        horizontal line:  the reflection over the horizontal lin e is
        <m>F_1</m>; the reflection over the line through vertices 1
        and 3 is <m>F_2</m>; the reflection over the vertical line is
        <m>F_3</m>; and the reflection over the line through vertices
        2 and 4 is <m>F_4</m>.  The lines in <xref ref="figure-d4" />
        are labelled with these reflection names. 
      </p>
      <p>
        That brings the total number of transformations to eight: the
        identity, three rotations, and four reflections. These eight
        are, in fact, the complete group.
      </p>
      <p>
        So I've constructed a group of symmetries of the square. I
        want to understand the structure of this group, so let me
        build the Caley table. 
      </p>
      <p>
        To make the Caley table, I need to know how to calculate the
        composition of elements. Since I am just working with
        vertices, I am going to describe each element of the group
        simply by how it moves the vertices. In <xref ref="figure-d4"
        />, I labelled the vertices. By showing where each vertex
        goes, I can describe each transformation. For example, the
        rotation <m>R_1</m> sends each vertex to the next vertex
        counterclockwise. I can represent this by the following vertex
        movements.
        <md>
          <mrow>
            1 \mapsto 2 
          </mrow>
          <mrow>
            2 \mapsto 3 
          </mrow>
          <mrow>
            3 \mapsto 4 
          </mrow>
          <mrow>
            4 \mapsto 1 
          </mrow>
        </md>
        The reflection <m>F_2</m> is over the line through vertices 1
        and 3. It fixes the two vertices which lie on that line and
        switches the other two. I can represent this by the following
        vertex movements. 
        <md>
          <mrow>
            1 \mapsto 1
          </mrow>
          <mrow>
            2 \mapsto 4
          </mrow>
          <mrow>  
            3 \mapsto 3
          </mrow>
          <mrow>
            4 \mapsto 2
          </mrow>
        </md>
        Now I can use these vertex movements to understand
        composition.  Consider <m>R_1 \circ F_2</m>. Since composition
        works from right to left, this is doing the reflection first,
        then the rotation.  I can put the two sets of vertex movements
        together.
        <md>
          <mrow>
            1 \mapsto 1 \mapsto 2 
          </mrow>
          <mrow>
            2 \mapsto 4 \mapsto 1
          </mrow>
          <mrow>  
            3 \mapsto 3 \mapsto 4
          </mrow>
          <mrow>
            4 \mapsto 2 \mapsto 3 
          </mrow>
        </md>
        Then I can ask: what is this transformation? This switches
        vertices <m>1</m> and <m>2</m>, and also switches the other
        two vertices. This must be reflected over the vertical line,
        which is <m>F_3</m>. Therefore, <m>R_1 \circ F_2 = F_3</m>.
        I've calculated a composition. What happens if I reverse the
        order: <m>F_2 \circ R_1</m>? Now the rotation happens first,
        but I can still calculate the vertex movements by doing the
        rotation then the reflection.
        <md>
          <mrow>
            1 \mapsto 2 \mapsto 4
          </mrow>
          <mrow>
            2 \mapsto 3 \mapsto 3
          </mrow>
          <mrow>
            3 \mapsto 4 \mapsto 2
          </mrow>
          <mrow>
            4 \mapsto 1 \mapsto 1
          </mrow>
        </md>
        This transformation switches vertices <m>1</m> and <m>4</m>,
        and also switches vertices <m>2</m> and <m>3</m>. It must be
        the reflection over the horizontal line, which is called
        <m>F_1</m>.  Therefore, <m>F_2 \circ R_1 = F_1</m>.
      </p>
      <p>
        In this way, I can calculate all the compositions and put them
        into a Caley table. 
      </p>
      <me>
        \begin{array}{c|cccccccc}
          \circ \amp \Id \amp R_1 \amp R_2 \amp R_3 \amp F_1 \amp F_2
          \amp F_3 \amp F_4 \\
          \hline
          \Id \amp \Id \amp R_1 \amp R_2 \amp R_3 \amp F_1 \amp F_2
          \amp F_3 \amp F_4 \\
          R_1 \amp R_1 \amp R_2 \amp R_3 \amp \Id \amp F_2 \amp F_3
          \amp F_4 \amp F_1 \\
          R_2 \amp R_2 \amp R_3 \amp \Id \amp R_1 \amp F_3 \amp F_4
          \amp F_1 \amp F_2 \\
          R_3 \amp R_3 \amp \Id \amp R_1 \amp R_2 \amp F_4 \amp F_1
          \amp F_2 \amp F_3 \\.
          F_1 \amp F_1 \amp F_4 \amp F_3 \amp F_2 \amp \Id \amp R_3
          \amp R_2 \amp R_1 \\
          F_2 \amp F_2 \amp F_1 \amp F_4 \amp F_3 \amp R_1 \amp \Id
          \amp R_3 \amp R_2 \\
          F_3 \amp F_3 \amp F_2 \amp F_1 \amp F_4 \amp R_2 \amp R_2
          \amp \Id \amp R_3 \\
          F_4 \amp F_4 \amp F_3 \amp F_2 \amp F_1 \amp R_3 \amp R_3
          \amp R_1 \amp \Id 
        \end{array}
      </me>
      <p>
        This is the group <m>D_4</m>.
      </p>
    </example>
    <p>
      In the same way, I can construct dihedral groups for any regular
      polygon, one for each <m>n</m>. Each of them have interesting
      structures and we will return to them frequently as important
      examples. I'll make a few general remarks here.
      <ul>
        <li>
          The group <m>D_n</m> (in the notation of this course) always
          has <m>2n</m> elements. These are: the identity, <m>n-1</m>
          rotation, and <m>n</m> reflections. 
        </li>
        <li>
          For even <m>n</m>, the reflections have the same pattern as
          the square: half of them are through two vertices and half
          are through the middle of two edges. For odd <m>n</m>, all
          the reflections are through one vertex and the middle of the
          opposite edge. This creates two patterns: it often makes
          sense to talk separately about the structure of the even
          dihedral groups or of the odd dihedral groups. 
        </li>
        <li>
          If you only consider the rotations, you get a cyclic group
          generated by the smallest angle of rotation. 
        </li>
      </ul>
    </p>
  </subsection>
  <subsection xml:id="subsection-matrix-groups">
    <title>Matrix Groups</title>
    <p>
      The reflections and rotations in dihedral groups can be realized
      as matrices, as long as you centre the polygon at the origin in
      <m>\RR^2</m>. The composition can then be calculated by matrix
      multiplication. In this way, the dihedral groups are matrix
      groups. But they are far from the only ones.
    </p>
    <p>
      When I say <sq>matrix group</sq>, I am always going to mean
      under the operation of matrix multiplication. It is possible
      to, say, add matrices component-wise, but that doens't really
      lead to interesting groups. Matrices and their multiplication
      lead to many, many important and well-studied groups. Let me run
      through a few of them here. 
    </p>
    <definition>
      <statement>
        <p>
          Here is a list of matrix groups. For these groups, I'll use
          <m>F</m> for the coefficients. (<sq><m>F</m></sq> is for field,
          which we'll talk about much later.) You can think the
          rationals, reals or complex numbers, but it's nice to state
          this in general. 
          <ul>
            <li>
              The <term>general linear group</term> is the group of
              all <m>n \times n</m> invertible matrices. Its notation
              is <m>GL(n,F)</m>. For real coefficients, often we just
              write <m>GL(n)</m>. 
            </li>
            <li>
              The <term>special linear group</term> is the group of
              all <m>n \times n</m> invertible matrices with
              determinant <m>1</m>. Its notation
              is <m>SL(n,F)</m>. For real coefficients, often we just
              write <m>SL(n)</m>. It is a fact (which I will not prove
              here), that <m>SL(n,\ZZ)</m> is also a group, even
              though integer coefficients do not create a group for
              most of the other definition in this list.
            </li>
            <li>
              The <term>orthogonal group</term> is the group of
              all <m>n \times n</m> orthogonal matrices. Its notation
              is <m>O(n,F)</m>. For real coefficients, often we just
              write <m>O(n)</m>. 
            </li>
            <li>
              The <term>special orthogonal group</term> is the group of
              all <m>n \times n</m> orthogonal matrices with
              determinant 1. Its notation
              is <m>SO(n,F)</m>. For real coefficients, often we just
              write <m>SO(n)</m>. 
            </li>
            <li>
              These groups, along with other similar matrix groups,
              are often referred to as the <term>classical
              groups</term>. The name refers to the fact that they
              were some of the first abstract groups to be studied. 
            </li>
          </ul>
        </p>
      </statement>
    </definition>
    <p>
      This is just scratching the surfuce, using the definition that
      can be reasonably made with the linear algebra background
      assumed for this course. There are many others, including the
      unitary groups and the sympletic groups. I know I said that this
      is a purely abstract course and I would forgo talk of applied
      mathematics, but it is worth noting that the special unitary
      groups, <m>SU(n)</m>, show up as the fundamental symmetry groups
      in various presentations and pieces of the Standard Model of
      elementary particles. 
    </p>
    <p>
      One of the reasons that matrix groups were studied so early in
      the theory is that they are often the first examples of
      nonabelian groups. Matrix multiplication is, in general,
      non-commutative. Many of the examples we've covered so far are
      abelian and abelian is familiar. However, much of the
      interesting group theory happens with non-abelian groups and
      matrices are a good entry to that world. 
    </p>
  </subsection>
  <subsection xml:id="subsection-symmetry-groups">
    <title>Symmetry Groups</title>
    <p>
      I just generalized the dihedral groups to a whole world of matrix
      groups. But I can generalize in another way as well. The
      dihedral groups are the groups of symmetric transformations
      (symmetries) of a shape. Such a group can be formed for
      basically any shape. As such, dihedral groups are the starting
      example also for this immense collection of groups. 
    </p>
    <p>
      This gets particularly interesting in higher dimensions. What is
      the symmetry group of the cube? Of the icosahedron? What about
      the symmetries of the tesseract? Or of the <m>7</m>-cross
      polytope? Allowing ourselves to consider geometry in higher
      dimensions really opens up enormous possibilities for finding
      interesting symmetry groups. 
    </p>
  </subsection>
</section>
